#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133612 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "df300955-830a-5347-9aeb-2b2b25f865c8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a One-Dimensional Subspace"}] :question/id #uuid "18068394-b64b-5bc8-a2da-b0db4085ecba" :question/math-academy-id "e-8729" :question/problem "Let $V = \\Bbb R_1[t],$ the vector space of polynomials of degree at most $1$ with real coefficients. Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n\\langle p(t),q(t) \\rangle = p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nfind the orthogonal projection of $p(t)=-5t-3$ onto the subspace spanned by the polynomial $q(t) =t-1.$" :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $p(t)$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{ q(t) \\}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{q(t)}\\,p(t) \n= \\dfrac{\\langle{p(t),q(t)}\\rangle}{\\langle{q(t),q(t)}\\rangle}\\,q(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1) \\\\  & =(-5t-3){|}_{0}\\cdot (t-1){|}_{0}+(-5t-3){|}_{1}\\cdot (t-1){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot (-1)+(-8)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =3+0 \\\\  & =3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨q(t),q(t)⟩ & =q(0)q(0)+q(1)q(1) \\\\  & =(t-1){|}_{0}\\cdot (t-1){|}_{0}+(t-1){|}_{1}\\cdot (t-1){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =1+0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the projection is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t) & =\\frac{⟨p(t),q(t)⟩}{⟨q(t),q(t)⟩}\\,q(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{1}\\cdot (t-1) \\\\  & =3(t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133613 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "39692b40-7d68-5ae5-9fe1-0f8b0b2e0d77" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "4e4a5db0-2ea0-542e-abdd-c5b3abfe2134" :question/math-academy-id "e-8730" :question/problem "Let $V = \\mathcal{C}[-1,1],$ the vector space of continuous functions over $[-1,1]$, and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n\n$$\n\\langle x(t),y(t) \\rangle =\\int_{-1}^{1} x(t) y(t) \\, \\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nf_1(t)=1,\\quad f_2(t)=\\sin \\pi t, \\quad g(t)=6t^2\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $S=\\text{Span}\\{f_1(t), f_2(t)\\}$ given that $f_1(t) \\perp f_2(t)$ with respect to the inner product, and that the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{f_1(t)\\}$ equals $2.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{f_1(t),f_2(t)\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,g(t) \n= \\textrm{proj}_{f_1(t)}\\,g(t) + \\textrm{proj}_{f_2(t)}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\nWe are given that $\\textrm{proj}_{f_1(t)}\\,g(t) = 2,$ so let's now find the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{f_2(t)\\}.$ This can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{f_2(t)}\\,g(t) \n= \\dfrac{\\langle{g(t),f_2(t)}\\rangle}{\\langle{f_2(t),f_2(t)}\\rangle}\\,f_2(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}g(t){f}_{2}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}6{t}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}6{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\sin (\\pi t)$ is an odd function, while $6t^2$ is even. Hence, their product $6 t^2 \\sin (\\pi t)$ must be an odd function. Now, since we have an integral over the interval $[-1,1],$ which is symmetric about the origin, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^{1}   6t^2 \\sin (\\pi t) \\, \\text{d}t = 0.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{f_2(t)}\\,g(t) = \\dfrac{0}{\\langle{f_2(t),f_2(t)}\\rangle} \\cdot  \\sin \\pi t = 0.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =2+0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133614 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "25e84fac-02c3-5111-98b3-bc67fe1ec9ab" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between a Vector and a Subspace"}] :question/id #uuid "d7541321-4042-54af-9b64-59f255a6f1a3" :question/math-academy-id "e-8733" :question/problem "Let $V = \\Bbb R_1[t],$ the vector space of polynomials of degree at most $1$ with real coefficients. Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n\\langle p(t), q(t) \\rangle = p(0)q(0) + p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nfind the distance between $p(t) = 4t + 2$ and the subspace spanned by $q(t) = t$ with respect to the inner product." :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $p(t)$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{ q(t) \\}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t) & =\\frac{⟨p(t),q(t)⟩}{⟨q(t),q(t)⟩}\\,q(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1)}{q(0)^{2}+q(1)^{2}}\\cdot q(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{(2)(0)+(6)(1)}{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\cdot t \\\\  & =6t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe distance between $p(t) = 4t + 2$ and the subspace spanned by $q(t) = t$ with respect to our inner product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖p(t)-{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖(4t+2)-6t‖ \\\\  & =‖-2t+2‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{⟨-2t+2,\\text{ }-2t+2⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨-2t+2,\\text{ }-2t+2⟩ & =(-2t+2){|}_{0}\\cdot (-2t+2){|}_{0}+(-2t+2){|}_{1}\\cdot (-2t+2){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(-2(0)+2)^{2}+(-2(1)+2)^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}+{0}^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $d = \\sqrt{4} = 2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329759 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "df300955-830a-5347-9aeb-2b2b25f865c8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a One-Dimensional Subspace"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec556b9e-e4a5-543e-bcdc-6bc4015a7b6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329761} {:answer/id #uuid "c145253d-0efc-588c-a5eb-8d2914e4d907" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9(3{t}^{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329762} {:answer/id #uuid "04f189f2-391d-5733-8a96-46a11ff2d315" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5(3t-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329763} {:answer/id #uuid "be2838d3-ad2d-5ac7-a8d6-be87e8e57fa1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5(3{t}^{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329764} {:answer/id #uuid "6eb618d0-e6ac-51ce-be3d-ae7d1f4c13aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329765}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be2838d3-ad2d-5ac7-a8d6-be87e8e57fa1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5(3{t}^{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329764} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e98d763-de02-5c03-b575-35d8592a9722" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329760}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3fe7eba6-b1a8-5d8c-8040-6976d04b7758" :question/math-academy-id "q-104865" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,1],$ the vector space of functions that are continuous over $[0,1].$ Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nfind the orthogonal projection of $f(t)=16{t}^{3}+2$ onto the subspace spanned by $g(t)=3{t}^{2}-1.$" :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $f(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{g(t)}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t)=\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),g(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(16{t}^{3}+2)\\cdot (3{t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(48{t}^{5}-16{t}^{3}+6{t}^{2}-2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[8{t}^{6}-4{t}^{4}+2{t}^{2}-2t]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(8-4+2-2)-0 \\\\  & =4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),g(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3{t}^{2}-1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{4}-6{t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\frac{9{t}^{5}}{5}-2{t}^{3}+t]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{9}{5}-2+1)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{(\\frac{4}{5})}\\cdot (3{t}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =5(3{t}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352847 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "39692b40-7d68-5ae5-9fe1-0f8b0b2e0d77" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "421c358a-2a34-5df1-a3f4-ea24e97b071a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4t-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352849} {:answer/id #uuid "57c16c71-d785-55a7-badc-dd726531e6ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8t-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352850} {:answer/id #uuid "a2a10e03-901e-5e5d-bc2c-d409a4634315" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4t+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352851} {:answer/id #uuid "b7fb3eea-a71a-5f62-98d2-a7fc0d9810ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352852} {:answer/id #uuid "7345c84e-0bf3-5007-85a9-02e8206cf377" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8t+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352853}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "421c358a-2a34-5df1-a3f4-ea24e97b071a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4t-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352849} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2e21f82-f24f-5792-b857-183cfbb32c2b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352848}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "37ca62b1-ea69-5fd7-b661-830063f158d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-104871" :question/problem "Let $V={R}_{1}[t],$ the vector space of real polynomials of degree at most $1,$ and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1).\n$$\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V:$\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=2t,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=t-1,\\,q(t)=4t-3\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto $S=\\text{Span}{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)}$ given that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the inner product, and that the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{p}_{1}(t)}$ equals $t.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the set ${{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,q(t)={\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{1}(t)}\\,q(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t).\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{1}(t)}\\,q(t)=t,$ so let's now find the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{p}_{2}(t)}.$ This can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t)=\\frac{⟨q(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩}{⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩}\\,{p}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨q(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & =q(0){p}_{2}(0)+q(1){p}_{2}(1) \\\\  & =(4t-3){|}_{0}\\cdot (t-1){|}_{0}+(4t-3){|}_{1}\\cdot (t-1){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & ={p}_{2}(0){p}_{2}(0)+{p}_{2}(1){p}_{2}(1) \\\\  & =(t-1)^{2}{|}_{0}+(t-1)^{2}{|}_{1} \\\\  & =(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t) & =\\frac{3}{1}\\cdot (t-1)=3t-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,q(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{1}(t)}\\,q(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t) \\\\  & =t+(3t-3) \\\\  & =4t-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133982 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "df300955-830a-5347-9aeb-2b2b25f865c8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a One-Dimensional Subspace"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae84596f-e22c-5163-8098-6f62fe1d9c65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130456} {:answer/id #uuid "d880bd13-c398-58c1-b54f-b927298d64f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130457} {:answer/id #uuid "3f67b46d-6850-5c01-b5ed-9cb3ad019420" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130458} {:answer/id #uuid "50a0b594-e148-5cca-95ce-f600cb38bb37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130459} {:answer/id #uuid "5f61cbda-71cc-5341-90e0-da1bc96974ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130460}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f67b46d-6850-5c01-b5ed-9cb3ad019420" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130458} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6c3db7d-e140-553f-9f2a-8667fd477988" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130455}] :question/id #uuid "0353ab94-587f-5f58-82a7-35f416a7a2f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-104944" :question/problem "Let $V = R_{1}[t]$, the vector space of polynomials of degree at most $1$ with real coefficients. Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as\n\n$⟨p(t), q(t)⟩ = p(0)q(0) + p(1)q(1)$,\nfind the orthogonal projection of $p(t) =-3t - 2$ onto the subspace spanned by the polynomial $q(t) = t$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329773 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "39692b40-7d68-5ae5-9fe1-0f8b0b2e0d77" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f82d6982-4fb8-52f3-a24e-8736d338b47f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329775} {:answer/id #uuid "f5ac8701-ccc2-515b-816a-7a2aa31a0582" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{t}^{2}+3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329776} {:answer/id #uuid "76be5757-02f7-5d18-ad1a-078740bdb63f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329777} {:answer/id #uuid "0642bf24-5352-5911-b934-cf9b0d0676f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t+t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329778} {:answer/id #uuid "3d147174-af74-51fb-ae8b-ced94b4ba111" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d147174-af74-51fb-ae8b-ced94b4ba111" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329779} :answer-field/id #uuid "24c268ec-8c6b-5d73-bf9a-155056132b1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329774}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c07bc9ef-08f9-5b31-9daf-46f1ad215cf8" :question/math-academy-id "q-105162" :question/problem "Let $V=C[-1,1],$ the vector space of continuous functions over $[-1,1]$, and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V:$\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)={t}^{2},\\,g(t)=\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $S=\\text{Span}{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)}$ given that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the inner product, and that the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{f}_{1}(t)}$ equals $3t.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the set ${{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t)={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)=3t,$ so let's now find the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{f}_{2}(t)}.$ This can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t)=\\frac{⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}{⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}\\,{f}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}g(t){f}_{2}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))\\cdot {t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)$ is an odd function, while ${t}^{2}$ is even. Hence, their product ${t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)$ must be an odd function. Now, since we have an integral over the interval $[-1,1],$ which is symmetric about the origin, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,\\text{d}t=0.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t)=\\frac{0}{⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}\\cdot {t}^{2}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =3t+0 \\\\  & =3t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329780 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "39692b40-7d68-5ae5-9fe1-0f8b0b2e0d77" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99dcd642-5fa4-5986-8a64-caafe3586a3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329782} {:answer/id #uuid "b33673f1-88af-5c61-a951-a4c3e1b5c796" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4t-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329783} {:answer/id #uuid "6d561531-76f6-59f3-a433-ab10416d35b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4t+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329784} {:answer/id #uuid "24776c90-b32b-5bdb-a3e4-efe023656906" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-t-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329785} {:answer/id #uuid "0ddef629-bbfa-567b-957c-7b01722117f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329786}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24776c90-b32b-5bdb-a3e4-efe023656906" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-t-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329785} :answer-field/id #uuid "5de5af44-6d73-5618-bc98-e2dcc9c1ac82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329781}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6a356727-a0fe-572f-be32-85750e64943b" :question/math-academy-id "q-105173" :question/problem "Let $V={R}_{1}[t],$ the vector space of real polynomials of degree at most $1,$ and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1).\n$$\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V:$\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=5t,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=-2t+2,\\,q(t)=-t-4\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto $S=\\text{Span}{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)}$ given that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the inner product, and that the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{p}_{1}(t)}$ equals $-5t.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the set ${{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,q(t)={\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{1}(t)}\\,q(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t).\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{1}(t)}\\,q(t)=-5t,$ so let's now find the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{p}_{2}(t)}.$ This can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t)=\\frac{⟨q(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩}{⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩}\\,{p}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨q(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & =q(0){p}_{2}(0)+q(1){p}_{2}(1) \\\\  & =(-t-4){|}_{0}\\cdot (-2t+2){|}_{0}+(-t-4){|}_{1}\\cdot (-2t+2){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(-4)\\cdot 2+(-5)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & ={p}_{2}(0){p}_{2}(0)+{p}_{2}(1){p}_{2}(1) \\\\  & =(-2t+2)^{2}{|}_{0}+(-2t+2)^{2}{|}_{1} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}+{0}^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t) & =\\frac{-8}{4}\\cdot (-2t+2)=4t-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,q(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{1}(t)}\\,q(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t) \\\\  & =-5t+(4t-4) \\\\  & =-t-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329794 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "25e84fac-02c3-5111-98b3-bc67fe1ec9ab" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between a Vector and a Subspace"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06c483de-f06a-54b1-a94a-580debc19ab7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329796} {:answer/id #uuid "aa68039c-6722-50d3-a689-f3b2aed812ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329797} {:answer/id #uuid "5dfa2e25-81ce-5924-970a-96d2a18faa26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329798} {:answer/id #uuid "8fc85b12-a821-5c07-94ec-cb320f330f46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329799} {:answer/id #uuid "9ce71407-c2ca-5832-a64c-511575a71f6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329800}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8fc85b12-a821-5c07-94ec-cb320f330f46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329799} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4e2d9bf-7e45-574a-9a72-53ef3116396c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329795}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9e6e65b2-e2c1-500c-950b-259a5f8738be" :question/math-academy-id "q-105461" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,1],$ the vector space of functions that are continuous over $[0,1].$ Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nfind the distance between $f(t)=3t$ and $g(t)=-3t+3$ with respect to the inner product." :question/worked-solution "The distance between $f(t)=3t$ and $g(t)=-3t+3$ with respect to the given inner product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖f(t)-g(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖3t-(-3t+3)‖ \\\\  & =‖6t-3‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{⟨6t-3,6t-3⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨6t-3,6t-3⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(6t-3)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(36{t}^{2}-36t+9)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[12{t}^{3}-18{t}^{2}+9t]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(12-18+9)-0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $d=\\sqrt{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352875 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "25e84fac-02c3-5111-98b3-bc67fe1ec9ab" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between a Vector and a Subspace"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b0b1a26-f579-5840-8668-4b207f166149" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{60}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352877} {:answer/id #uuid "7a22a883-5a19-5879-8980-7727104b6537" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{30}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352878} {:answer/id #uuid "d18dac2c-4f62-52b8-a102-0166403e86c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{40}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352879} {:answer/id #uuid "e99d78f5-7d23-5860-b726-f770242d40bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352880} {:answer/id #uuid "796c4214-08a5-5537-873f-f07374362cf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352881}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a22a883-5a19-5879-8980-7727104b6537" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{30}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352878} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9f18baf-03c5-50fe-8c56-32209d6d9081" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352876}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ca703624-c22b-5cc8-9120-0825031366fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-105488" :question/problem "Let $V={R}_{2}[t],$ the vector space of polynomials of degree at most $2$ with real coefficients. Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nfind the distance between $p(t)=5t$ and the subspace spanned by $q(t)=t-1$ with respect to the inner product." :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $p(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{q(t)}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t) & =\\frac{⟨p(t),q(t)⟩}{⟨q(t),q(t)⟩}\\,q(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1)}{q(-1)q(-1)+q(0)q(0)+q(1)q(1)}\\cdot q(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\sum }_{i\\in {-1,0,1}}(5t){|}_{t=i}\\cdot (t-1){|}_{t=i}}{{\\sum }_{i\\in {-1,0,1}}(t-1){|}_{t=i}\\cdot (t-1){|}_{t=i}}\\cdot (t-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{(-5)\\cdot (-2)+0\\cdot (-1)+5\\cdot 0}{(-2)\\cdot (-2)+(-1)\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot 0}\\cdot (t-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{5}\\cdot (t-1) \\\\  & =2(t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe distance between $p(t)=5t$ and the subspace spanned by $q(t)=t-1$ with respect to our inner product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖p(t)-{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖5t-2(t-1)‖ \\\\  & =‖3t+2‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{⟨3t+2,3t+2⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨3t+2,3t+2⟩ & ={\\sum }_{i\\in {-1,0,1}}(3t+2){|}_{t=i}\\cdot (3t+2){|}_{t=i} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 2+5\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =1+4+25 \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $d=\\sqrt{30}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329808 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "25e84fac-02c3-5111-98b3-bc67fe1ec9ab" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between a Vector and a Subspace"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe6ee12f-1223-55a0-aeb4-f87a3e2e3cc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329810} {:answer/id #uuid "732ee3bd-8c0a-551e-ab05-b2efa5cedba1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329811} {:answer/id #uuid "d519eac8-d7a1-5bf5-bf48-fac18a99108a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329812} {:answer/id #uuid "cd395293-0d16-5163-94cc-bb265ab83dc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329813} {:answer/id #uuid "29d97a5d-9e4a-5692-ac7e-c79b1797a2dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329814}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d519eac8-d7a1-5bf5-bf48-fac18a99108a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329812} :answer-field/id #uuid "c87e86e6-e8d0-51e8-bf93-3336aecaeb74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329809}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "427e7cc7-9f36-51c5-83a8-b85a2e8396b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-105494" :question/problem "Let $V={R}_{2}[t],$ the vector space of polynomials of degree at most $2$ with real coefficients. Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nfind the distance between $p(t)=7{t}^{2}-t$ and the subspace spanned by $q(t)=4{t}^{2}-4t$ with respect to the inner product." :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $p(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{q(t)}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t) & =\\frac{⟨p(t),q(t)⟩}{⟨q(t),q(t)⟩}\\,q(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1)}{q(-1)q(-1)+q(0)q(0)+q(1)q(1)}\\cdot q(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\sum }_{i\\in {-1,0,1}}(7{t}^{2}-t){|}_{t=i}\\cdot (4{t}^{2}-4t){|}_{t=i}}{{\\sum }_{i\\in {-1,0,1}}(4{t}^{2}-4t){|}_{t=i}\\cdot (4{t}^{2}-4t){|}_{t=i}}\\cdot (4{t}^{2}-4t) \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\cdot 8+0\\cdot 0+6\\cdot 0}{8\\cdot 8+0\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 0}\\cdot (4{t}^{2}-4t) \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{64}\\cdot (4{t}^{2}-4t) \\\\  & =4{t}^{2}-4t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe distance between $p(t)=7{t}^{2}-t$ and the subspace spanned by $q(t)=4{t}^{2}-4t$ with respect to our inner product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖p(t)-{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖7{t}^{2}-t-(4{t}^{2}-4t)‖ \\\\  & =‖3{t}^{2}+3t‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{⟨3{t}^{2}+3t,3{t}^{2}+3t⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨3{t}^{2}+3t,3{t}^{2}+3t⟩ & ={\\sum }_{i\\in {-1,0,1}}(3{t}^{2}+3t){|}_{t=i}\\cdot (3{t}^{2}+3t){|}_{t=i} \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 0+6\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =0+0+36 \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $d=\\sqrt{36}=6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134483 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "df300955-830a-5347-9aeb-2b2b25f865c8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a One-Dimensional Subspace"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c9f8e867-8753-5910-96da-9aa566c13fe0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6t - 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133341} {:answer/id #uuid "0953abb6-f6f7-5da2-a3da-74a419f01278" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133342} {:answer/id #uuid "0f9a9ced-82f5-586f-83d7-47d1211e6963" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133343} {:answer/id #uuid "5b8aec29-252e-57ec-a072-62b2226f1d2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12t - 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133344} {:answer/id #uuid "6c26e37d-2b91-51cd-8b50-542f8ff915f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133345}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c26e37d-2b91-51cd-8b50-542f8ff915f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133345} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd11813b-edf9-557c-9df2-cac8504a1540" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133340}] :question/id #uuid "bf30346a-5df6-5b01-8f7e-1f3d45dab03f" :question/math-academy-id "q-98694" :question/problem "Let $V = C[0, 1]$, the vector space of functions that are continuous over $[0, 1]$. Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as\n\n$⟨x(t), y(t)⟩ = ∫_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)dt$,\nfind the orthogonal projection of $f(t) = 2t^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by $g(t) = 6t - 3$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134484 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "25e84fac-02c3-5111-98b3-bc67fe1ec9ab" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between a Vector and a Subspace"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97f87aab-fa20-5534-ba98-4d817c88f286" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133347} {:answer/id #uuid "a5f8376a-778e-5c85-b36b-6569ca09d3db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133348} {:answer/id #uuid "14c88388-e221-5b54-8dc0-34ca2d827a35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133349} {:answer/id #uuid "4c3a24a4-eb3d-51a4-9f78-1ac811078ed6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133350} {:answer/id #uuid "57a36fb6-39f9-5be6-a938-85d1542f79d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133351}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97f87aab-fa20-5534-ba98-4d817c88f286" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133347} :answer-field/id #uuid "442d8234-d5d2-57c7-aff2-a9f143ac1c6b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133346}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "526aae2e-3eab-5216-b385-aa02beed49e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-98750" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,1],$ the vector space of functions that are continuous over $[0,1].$ Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nfind the distance between $f(t)=2t$ and the subspace spanned by $g(t)=6t-3$ with respect to the inner product." :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $f(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{g(t)}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\int }_{0}^{1}2t\\cdot (6t-3)\\,\\text{d}t}{{\\int }_{0}^{1}(6t-3)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t}\\cdot (6t-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\int }_{0}^{1}(12{t}^{2}-6t)\\,\\text{d}t}{{\\int }_{0}^{1}(36{t}^{2}-36t+9)\\,\\text{d}t}\\cdot (6t-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{{[4{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}]}_{0}^{1}}{{[12{t}^{3}-18{t}^{2}+9t]}_{0}^{1}}\\cdot (6t-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{(4-3)-0}{(12-18+9)-0}\\cdot (6t-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (6t-3) \\\\  & =2t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe distance between $f(t)=2t$ and the subspace spanned by $g(t)=6t-3$ with respect to our inner product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖f(t)-{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖2t-(2t-1)‖ \\\\  & =‖1‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{⟨1,1⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨1,1⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}1\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =t{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1-0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $d=\\sqrt{1}=1.$"}]}
